1. TOW → Knowledge Base Integration

* John liked the integration/scaffolding already created and said it is the right starting point.
* However, do not broadly roll out/push the TOW–KB crossover yet because the Knowledge Base is not stable/mature enough.
* First stabilize the KB: permissions, controls, bugs, architecture, and dead code.
* Existing previously agreed TOW changes can continue through Staging → UAT → Production.

2. Knowledge Base Navigation

* Add Knowledge as its own section in the TOW navigation.
* It should sit between the core TOW functionality and Admin, separated visually.
* “Knowledge” should represent the entire organizational Knowledge Base, not only TOW application documentation.

3. Knowledge Can Be Linked to TOW Items

John likes the ability to associate relevant KB knowledge with TOW work.

For example, if there is a TOW issue involving EXL, users could reference/link a KB article describing the EXL vendor relationship, agreements, expectations, or related policies.

So KB is not just application training; it should eventually represent broader organizational knowledge.

4. GitHub / CI-CD → Automatic Knowledge Articles

This is an important future workflow John wants.

When a feature is developed:

PRD/Markdown docs → PR/GitHub workflow → deployment/release → generate/update an end-user Knowledge Base article → approval/publishing

The goal is to avoid relying on tribal knowledge when application changes reach production.

John’s long-term vision is that developers/users should be able to use tools such as Cursor/Claude with standardized templates to help create KB articles rather than manually authoring everything.

5. Knowledge Base Permissions – High Priority

KB currently needs stronger permissioning/governance.

The team needs to establish clearly:

* Who can view knowledge.
* Who can author/create articles.
* Who can edit articles.
* Who can delete/manage articles.
* How user/team permissions work across the KB hierarchy.

Because TOW and KB share the monorepo/user ecosystem, John suggested leveraging the existing TOW user model to help determine KB permissions on a per-user basis.

6. PDP/PEP – User Permission UI

For the recently implemented additional/user-specific permissions, John requested a UI change:

Current: searchable multi-select permissions.

Wanted: clear True/False checkbox list on the User Edit/Profile page.

For example:

☑ Delete Goal
☑ Archive Goal
☐ Approve Transfer

The existing role/system permissions remain the baseline, with additional individual permissions layered on top.

John also emphasized that KB permissions have not yet been incorporated into the current PDP/PEP work and will require additional design because KB permissions can have a hierarchy/waterfall involving both users and teams.

7. Clean Up Knowledge Base / Dead Code

John believes the KB contains significant old/dead or unfinished functionality.

He specifically mentioned reviewing/removing things such as:

* Nonfunctional/old Chatbot functionality.
* Unused/incomplete File Upload functionality.
* Other dead code and unfinished scaffolding.

The objective is to remove misleading functionality before broader users start testing, otherwise users will report bugs against features that were never truly operational.

8. Use Existing GitHub Research & Plans

John has already done significant architecture research and planning.

The team should carefully review the repository’s:

* docs
* research documents
* implementation plans

These already contain thinking around areas such as schema foundation, pipelines/retrieval, MCP integrations, dead-code cleanup, and broader KB architecture.

John’s direction is to use those documents as the basis for implementation rather than designing everything again from scratch.

9. Knowledge Base as the Source for AI Agents

Longer term, John wants agents such as the existing IT-ticketing / HR LangSmith use cases to retrieve knowledge from the Knowledge Base rather than directly depending on scattered:

Word documents / PDFs / shared drives

Desired direction:

Existing documents → structured KB articles → governed/approved KB → agents query relevant KB sections

The team can create POCs around these use cases to help determine the correct KB retrieval/MCP architecture.

10. Two TOW Functionalities to Stabilize Before New TOW Features

Before doing additional new TOW features, John specifically asked the team to investigate two historically inconsistent areas.

Shared/Public/Private Lists & Settings: Review public/private/shared list visibility, team relationships, who can create/access lists, and why users sometimes cannot see expected shared lists/items. John wants end-to-end stability, not just one isolated bug fix.

Notifications: Review the full notification lifecycle. Notifications should reliably trigger for scenarios such as to-do assignment, pending movements, comments/posts on goals/items, and other actionable events. Indicators should appear consistently in both the relevant navigation/menu areas and top notification icon/bell.

11. New Staging/UAT Process

John plans to establish a standard UAT group led by Cole Phelps, with approximately six volunteer users.

Expected release process:

Development → Staging → Send clear change/test summary to Cole + UAT users → UAT → Approval/green light → Production

Cole will become an important power UAT contact for staging, and John indicated he will largely rely on this group’s validation before production pushes.

Final Priority Order from John

1. Move already-completed TOW changes through Staging → UAT → Production.

2. Before adding more TOW features, stabilize Lists/Settings and Notifications.

3. Keep the existing TOW–KB integration scaffolding, but do not broadly expose/deepen the integration until KB is stable.

4. Make the Knowledge Base the primary engineering focus: clean dead code, establish permissions/governance, follow the existing repo research/implementation plans, strengthen schema/infrastructure/retrieval/MCP foundations, and explore GitHub/CI-CD-driven article generation.

5. Later, use the stable KB as the governed knowledge source for TOW workflows and LangSmith/AI agents.

One-line summary for the team

John is happy with the TOW–KB integration foundation, but wants us to stop expanding the crossover for now, stabilize and properly build the Knowledge Base first, fix TOW Lists/Notifications, establish stronger KB permissions and governance, clean dead code, follow the existing GitHub architecture plans, and use Cole’s UAT group before production releases.
